  "question": {
    "text": "A superconducting loop of the radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> has self-inductance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math>. A uniform and the constant magnetic field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> is applied perpendicular to the plane of the loop. Initially current in this loop is zero. The loop is rotated by <math><mn>180</mn><mo>°</mo></math>. The current in the loop after the rotation is",
    "image": null
  },
